 - **[React](https://react.dev/)**: Used for UI components.
 - **[Tailwind CSS](https://tailwindcss.com/)**: For styling the application.
 - **[Astro DB](https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/database/)**: Powers the comments feature, using a libSQL-compatible database.
+
+## Account security and passkeys
+
+Open `/devtools-times/account/security`, or choose **My account** in the header.
+Sign in with an existing Times username and password to create, view, rename, or
+remove your passkeys. The password is verified by the existing login service;
+this app stores only the local account session, profile, and public credentials.
+Registered passkeys can also be used on the sign-in page.
+
+Passkeys require HTTPS in production (localhost works for development). RP IDs
+are bound to the request hostname, so keep a consistent public hostname. Device
+support is detected before offering registration; existing credentials remain
+manageable on browsers that do not support creating passkeys. Password manager
+synchronization uses feature-detected WebAuthn Signal methods.
+
+Credentials are persisted in `.data/passkeys.json`, separately from sessions.
+Set `PASSKEY_STORE_PATH` to a persistent volume location when deploying. The
+file store supports a single Node process; replace it with a transactional shared
+database before running multiple replicas. Provider metadata is a small local
+subset of the [passkey-authenticator-aaguids registry](https://github.com/passkeydeveloper/passkey-authenticator-aaguids);
+unrecognized or zero AAGUIDs receive a generic provider label.
+
+Run `pnpm test:passkeys` to build and test registration, authentication, profile
+updates, credential management, replay rejection, and cross-account isolation.
+The HTTP integration test uses synthetic signed WebAuthn responses and mocks
+only the external password login service; it does not require a browser or make
+changes to real accounts.
